On Mon, 5 Apr 2010, Robin Lee Powell wrote:

> http://teddyb.org/robin/tiki-index.php?page=Lojban%3A+You're+Doing+It+Wrong

Very good essay.  Think French -- a long time ago Lojban Central described 
its function to be like the French Academy, which speaks authoritatively 
about how the language is defined (despite impure speakers de l'etranger 
debasing it).  Because Lojban is a "logical language" it even more needs 
such an authoritative voice, and Lojban Central should return to that role.

"Let usage decide" is fine for a natural language with no pretense of being 
designed, but abdicating the design role -- "a consistent failure to 
govern", quoted from the political sphere -- serves our language poorly.

> (2) agree/disagree answer to the following question, after you've read
> (as much as you wish to) of the essay:
>
>  I would like Lojban to remain as close as it possibly can to its
>  current state, regardless of whether I or a group of experienced
>  Lojbanists see that improvements could be made.

Disagree.  If an area needs improvement we ought to improve it, and 
sometimes the change cannot be guaranteed to be backward-compatible in all 
cases.  On the other hand, Lojbanists' investment in the language is a very 
valuable resource not to be wasted, and wholesale revisions are not 
welcome.  The revision from Loglan to Lojban was essential for political 
reasons, but I hope we won't see anything like that again.

